---
import {
  ACCOUNT_TOOL_DEFINITIONS,
  ACCOUNT_TOOL_NAMES,
  AGENT_PROMPT_NAME,
  AGENT_PROMPT_TOOL,
  TOOL_DEFINITIONS,
  TOOL_NAMES,
  toolFields,
} from "@exeora/protocol";
import Doc from "../../layouts/Doc.astro";

/**
 * The tool reference, generated from the contract rather than written beside it.
 *
 * `TOOL_DEFINITIONS` is what the gateway advertises to the client and what the
 * executor validates arguments against. Reading it here at build time is what
 * stops this page from being the third description of the same thing, and the
 * one that quietly goes out of date. Adding a tool publishes its reference.
 */

const tools = TOOL_NAMES.map((name) => ({
  name,
  title: TOOL_DEFINITIONS[name].title,
  description: TOOL_DEFINITIONS[name].description,
  readOnly: TOOL_DEFINITIONS[name].readOnly,
  fields: toolFields(name),
}));

const accountTools = ACCOUNT_TOOL_NAMES.map((name) => ({
  name,
  title: ACCOUNT_TOOL_DEFINITIONS[name].title,
  description: ACCOUNT_TOOL_DEFINITIONS[name].description,
  readOnly: ACCOUNT_TOOL_DEFINITIONS[name].readOnly,
  fields: toolFields(name),
}));
---

<Doc
  title="Tools"
  description="What an agent connected to a project can do, and exactly what each tool takes."
  canonicalPath="/docs/tools/"
>
  <p>
    Every path is interpreted relative to the project root and confined to it before anything
    touches the disk. An absolute path, or one that climbs out with <code>..</code>, is refused with
    <code>PATH_ESCAPE</code> rather than resolved.
  </p>

  <p>
    Which of these a project actually offers is up to its policy: see{" "}
    <a href="/docs/policy/">what a project allows</a>. Tools marked <strong>read only</strong> change
    nothing, which is what a project set to read only is left with, and what is never interrupted by
    a confirmation prompt.
  </p>

  <p>
    This page is generated from the same definitions the gateway advertises and the executor
    validates against, so it cannot describe a tool that does not exist or miss an argument that
    does.
  </p>

  <p>
    On the account URL, <code>https://exeora.dev/mcp</code>, every tool below also takes an optional
    <code>project</code>, naming the project to run that one call in without changing the active one.
    A project's own URL has no such argument: it carries its project in the path, and offering a way
    to name another is exactly what it does not do.
  </p>

  {
    tools.map((tool) => (
      <section>
        <h2 id={tool.name}>
          <code>{tool.name}</code>
        </h2>

        <p>
          <strong>{tool.title}</strong>
          {tool.readOnly && <span class="text-foreground-faint"> · read only</span>}
        </p>

        <p>{tool.description}</p>

        {tool.fields.length > 0 && (
          <div class="overflow-x-auto">
            <table>
              <thead>
                <tr>
                  <th>Argument</th>
                  <th>Type</th>
                  <th>What it means</th>
                </tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody>
                {tool.fields.map((field) => (
                  <tr>
                    <td>
                      <code>{field.name}</code>
                      {!field.required && <span class="text-foreground-faint"> optional</span>}
                    </td>
                    <td>
                      <code>{field.type}</code>
                    </td>
                    <td>{field.description}</td>
                  </tr>
                ))}
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>
        )}
      </section>
    ))
  }

  <h2 id="choosing-a-project">Choosing a project</h2>

  <p>
    These three exist only on the account URL, where one connection covers several projects and
    something has to say which one a call lands in. They are answered by the gateway and never reach
    a machine, so no project policy applies to them.
  </p>

  <p>
    With one project granted, there is nothing to choose and calls go there. With more, the first
    call that has not been told where to go is refused with <code>NO_ACTIVE_PROJECT</code>, which
    names these tools. The choice belongs to the client rather than to a conversation, so it outlives
    the one that made it and is shared with any other conversation open in the same client.
  </p>

  {
    accountTools.map((tool) => (
      <section>
        <h2 id={tool.name}>
          <code>{tool.name}</code>
        </h2>

        <p>
          <strong>{tool.title}</strong>
          {tool.readOnly && <span class="text-foreground-faint"> · read only</span>}
          <span class="text-foreground-faint"> · account URL only</span>
        </p>

        <p>{tool.description}</p>

        {tool.fields.length > 0 && (
          <div class="overflow-x-auto">
            <table>
              <thead>
                <tr>
                  <th>Argument</th>
                  <th>Type</th>
                  <th>What it means</th>
                </tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody>
                {tool.fields.map((field) => (
                  <tr>
                    <td>
                      <code>{field.name}</code>
                      {!field.required && <span class="text-foreground-faint"> optional</span>}
                    </td>
                    <td>
                      <code>{field.type}</code>
                    </td>
                    <td>{field.description}</td>
                  </tr>
                ))}
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>
        )}
      </section>
    ))
  }

  <h2 id="reading-the-manual">Reading the manual</h2>

  <p>
    One more, on both URLs, taking no arguments and reaching no machine.
  </p>

  <section>
    <h2 id={AGENT_PROMPT_TOOL.name}>
      <code>{AGENT_PROMPT_TOOL.name}</code>
    </h2>

    <p>
      <strong>{AGENT_PROMPT_TOOL.title}</strong>
      <span class="text-foreground-faint"> · read only</span>
    </p>

    <p>{AGENT_PROMPT_TOOL.description}</p>

    <p>
      It returns a constant from the gateway, so it answers with the machine asleep and no project
      policy applies to it. The same text arrives as the <code>{AGENT_PROMPT_NAME}</code> prompt, and
      a shorter form of it in the handshake. See <a href="/docs/agent-prompt/">the agent prompt</a>.
    </p>
  </section>
</Doc>
